    Section(
      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content:
          'A 65-year-old right-handed male is referred to the cancer rehabilitation clinic by his colorectal surgeon 6 weeks prior to planned open right hemicolectomy for a newly diagnosed T3N1M0 (Stage III) adenocarcinoma of the ascending colon. He was found to have the mass during a screening colonoscopy prompted by 3 months of fatigue and unintentional 7 kg weight loss. He describes profound exhaustion — he can barely walk to his mailbox without needing to rest and has stopped walking his dog, an activity he previously enjoyed daily. He sleeps 10-11 hours per night but wakes unrefreshed. He is a retired mechanical engineer whose wife died of pancreatic cancer 18 months ago. He lives alone and his only child lives 4 hours away. When the surgeon mentioned rehabilitation before surgery, the patient responded: "Why bother exercising if I might die anyway?"',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content:
          '- **Oncologic History**: Ascending colon adenocarcinoma T3N1M0, diagnosed 2 weeks ago via colonoscopy with biopsy. CT chest/abdomen/pelvis shows no distant metastases. CEA elevated at 8.4 ng/mL. Planned for open right hemicolectomy in 6 weeks, followed by adjuvant FOLFOX chemotherapy.\n- **Fatigue Characterization**: Onset 3 months ago, progressive. BFI (Brief Fatigue Inventory) worst fatigue 8/10, average fatigue 7/10, interference with daily activities 8/10. Not relieved by rest. Worse in afternoon. No clear exacerbating factors beyond activity.\n- **Weight Loss**: 82 kg to 75 kg over 3 months (8.5% loss). Decreased appetite. Eating 1-2 small meals/day. No nausea or vomiting.\n- **Functional Decline**: 6-minute walk test equivalent — estimates he can walk about 5 minutes on flat ground before needing to stop. Previously walked 30-45 minutes daily with his dog. Stopped 2 months ago. Difficulty climbing the 14 stairs to his bedroom — now sleeps on the couch some nights. Grip strength declining — has trouble opening jars.\n- **Sleep**: 10-11 hours/night, unrefreshed. No snoring per self-report (lives alone). Denies restless legs. Takes diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly for sleep (started after wife\'s death).\n- **Mood**: PHQ-9 score 15 (moderately severe depression). GAD-7 score 14 (moderate anxiety). Grief from wife\'s death 18 months ago — reports it was \'the same cancer\' (she had pancreatic adenocarcinoma). Fears the same outcome.\n- **PMH**: Hypertension, type 2 diabetes (HbA1c 7.8%), hyperlipidemia, mild COPD (former smoker, 30 pack-years, quit 10 years ago), osteoarthritis bilateral knees\n- **Medications**: Metformin 1000 mg BID, amlodipine 5 mg daily, atorvastatin 40 mg daily, diphenhydramine 50 mg QHS, tiotropium inhaler daily\n- **Social History**: Widower, lives alone in two-story house. One adult daughter (nurse) lives 4 hours away. Retired. Limited social network since wife\'s death — stopped attending their couples\' social groups. Has a dog (golden retriever) he describes as \'my only companion.\'\n- **Review of Systems**: Fatigue, anorexia, 7 kg weight loss, depressed mood, anxiety, knee pain with stairs, mild dyspnea on exertion, occasional loose stools',
