    Section(
      title: '+ Construct a systematic differential diagnosis for this patient\'s cancer-related fatigue, identifying reversible contributors.',
      content:
          '**Cancer-Related Fatigue (CRF) — Systematic Differential**:\n\nCRF is multifactorial. The NCCN guidelines recommend screening for **treatable contributing factors** before attributing fatigue solely to the cancer itself.\n\n**Reversible Contributors Identified in This Patient**:\n\n1. **Anemia** (Hgb 10.2 g/dL):\n- Iron deficiency anemia from chronic GI blood loss (colon cancer). Low iron, low ferritin, elevated TIBC confirm iron deficiency.\n- **Treatment**: IV iron infusion (ferric carboxymaltose or iron sucrose) — preferred over oral iron given malabsorption risk and time pressure before surgery. Target Hgb >12 before surgery if possible.\n\n2. **Depression** (PHQ-9 = 15):\n- Moderately severe depression with complicated grief (wife\'s death from cancer). Fatigue, hypersomnia, anorexia, and social withdrawal are overlapping symptoms.\n- **Treatment**: SSRI initiation (sertraline or escitalopram), grief counseling referral, consider psychiatry consultation given severity.\n\n3. **Deconditioning/Sarcopenia**:\n- 6MWT 285 m (severely reduced), grip strength below sarcopenia threshold, chair stand test impaired. Progressive functional decline over 3 months from inactivity cycle (fatigue leads to less activity, which worsens fatigue).\n- **Treatment**: Structured exercise program (the core of prehabilitation).\n\n4. **Subclinical Hypothyroidism** (TSH 6.8):\n- TSH mildly elevated. While mild, hypothyroidism exacerbates fatigue. In the context of surgery, treating is reasonable.\n- **Treatment**: Start low-dose levothyroxine 25-50 mcg daily. Recheck TSH in 6 weeks.\n\n5. **Malnutrition/Cachexia**:\n- 8.5% weight loss in 3 months, low albumin (3.2), low prealbumin (14), reduced caloric intake. Meets criteria for cancer cachexia (>5% weight loss in 6 months with underlying disease).\n- **Treatment**: Nutritional optimization (see management plan).\n\n6. **Vitamin D Deficiency** (18 ng/mL):\n- Contributes to fatigue, weakness, and mood disturbance.\n- **Treatment**: Vitamin D3 50,000 IU weekly x 8 weeks, then 2000 IU daily maintenance.\n\n7. **Medication Side Effect**:\n- **Diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly** — anticholinergic, causes next-day sedation and cognitive dulling. Major contributor to unrefreshed sleep and daytime fatigue in older adults.\n- **Treatment**: Discontinue. Replace with sleep hygiene education or low-dose melatonin.\n\n8. **Uncontrolled Diabetes** (HbA1c 7.8%):\n- Hyperglycemia contributes to fatigue. Perioperative glycemic control also reduces surgical complications.\n- **Treatment**: Optimize metformin, consider adding a second agent. Endocrinology consultation.\n\n9. **Sleep Disorder**:\n- Hypersomnia (10-11 hours) with unrefreshed sleep. Rule out obstructive sleep apnea (BMI now 25, but may have had higher BMI previously, male, age). Consider sleep study if time permits.\n\n10. **COPD/Mild Hypoxia** (SpO2 94%):\n- Borderline oxygen saturation at rest. May desaturate further with exertion. Ensure tiotropium compliance, consider pulmonary function testing.\n\n**Cancer Itself**: Tumor-related cytokine activation (IL-6, TNF-alpha) causes central fatigue. This is the non-reversible component that will improve after tumor resection.',
