    Section(
      title: '+ What is the evidence base for cancer prehabilitation, and how does it differ from standard preoperative optimization?',
      content:
          '**Cancer Prehabilitation — Evidence Base**:\n\n**Definition**: Prehabilitation is a **proactive, multimodal intervention** initiated between cancer diagnosis and treatment (surgery, chemotherapy, or radiation) designed to optimize physical and psychological function, build physiologic reserve, and improve treatment tolerance and recovery.\n\n**Key Distinction from Standard Preoperative Optimization**:\n- Standard preoperative care focuses on medical optimization (controlling comorbidities, correcting labs, NPO protocols)\n- Prehabilitation adds **structured exercise, nutritional supplementation, and psychological support** as formal therapeutic interventions in the pre-treatment window\n\n**Evidence for Colorectal Cancer Prehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Functional Capacity**: Multiple RCTs demonstrate that 4-6 weeks of prehabilitation improves 6MWT distance by 25-40 meters and VO2 peak by 1.5-2.0 mL/kg/min compared to controls. Patients who enter surgery with higher fitness levels recover faster.\n\n2. **Postoperative Complications**: Systematic reviews and meta-analyses show a **30-50% reduction in overall postoperative complications** (particularly pulmonary complications, wound infections, and ileus) in prehabilitated patients undergoing major abdominal surgery.\n\n3. **Length of Stay**: Prehabilitation is associated with **1.5-2.5 day shorter hospital stays** after colorectal surgery.\n\n4. **Functional Recovery**: Prehabilitated patients return to baseline function **4-6 weeks faster** than controls. Without prehabilitation, many patients never return to their pre-diagnosis function level.\n\n5. **The "Dip and Recovery" Model**: Surgery causes a predictable decline in function (the "dip"). Patients who start at a higher functional level (through prehabilitation) dip to a less severe nadir and recover to a higher plateau. Patients who are deconditioned at surgery have less reserve, dip below the threshold for complications, and may never fully recover.\n\n**Multimodal Prehabilitation Components (Triad)**:\n1. **Exercise**: Aerobic + resistance training (most critical component)\n2. **Nutrition**: Protein supplementation + caloric optimization\n3. **Psychological**: Anxiety reduction + coping skills + motivation\n\n**Cost-Effectiveness**: Multiple analyses show prehabilitation is cost-effective due to reduced complications, shorter ICU and hospital stays, and faster return to independence. The estimated savings are \$2,000-5,000 per patient in reduced acute care costs.',
    ),
