    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a comprehensive 6-week prehabilitation program for this patient addressing exercise, nutrition, and psychological preparation.',
      content:
          '**6-Week Multimodal Prehabilitation Program**:\n\n**EXERCISE PRESCRIPTION** (supervised 3x/week + home program):\n\n*Aerobic Component*:\n- **Weeks 1-2**: Walk 10-15 minutes at moderate intensity (RPE 3-4/10 or "can talk but not sing"). Start with flat terrain. Include 3-minute warm-up and cool-down. Daily frequency encouraged, even in 5-minute bouts.\n- **Weeks 3-4**: Progress to 20-25 minutes. Introduce inclines or increased pace. Consider stationary cycling to reduce knee stress. Target RPE 4-5/10.\n- **Weeks 5-6**: 30 minutes moderate intensity. Include interval training: 2-minute moderate / 1-minute vigorous alternating. Monitor SpO2 with exertion — supplemental O2 if desaturates below 88%.\n- **Goal**: Improve 6MWT by 30+ meters and reduce dyspnea with daily activities.\n\n*Resistance Component*:\n- **Weeks 1-2**: Bodyweight exercises — sit-to-stand (progress to chair squats), wall push-ups, seated rows with resistance band, standing hip abduction. 2 sets of 8-10 repetitions. Rest 60-90 seconds between sets.\n- **Weeks 3-4**: Add light dumbbells (2-5 lbs) or increase resistance band tension. Include leg press, chest press, lat pulldown, bicep curls. 2-3 sets of 10-12 repetitions.\n- **Weeks 5-6**: Progress to moderate resistance. Focus on functional movements — carrying weighted bags (grocery simulation), stair climbing with progression, floor-to-stand transfers.\n- **Goal**: Improve grip strength by 4-5 kg, chair stand test >10 repetitions.\n\n*Inspiratory Muscle Training*:\n- Threshold IMT device at 30% of maximal inspiratory pressure (MIP).\n- 30 breaths, 2 times daily.\n- Reduces postoperative pulmonary complications by 50% in abdominal surgery patients.\n\n**NUTRITIONAL OPTIMIZATION** (dietitian-directed):\n\n1. **Caloric needs**: 30-35 kcal/kg/day (2250-2625 kcal/day). Emphasize calorie-dense foods given poor appetite.\n2. **Protein**: 1.5 g/kg/day (112 g/day). Whey protein supplement 20-30 g twice daily between meals. Leucine-enriched supplements preferred for muscle protein synthesis.\n3. **Iron repletion**: IV ferric carboxymaltose 750 mg x 2 doses (day 1 and day 7). Recheck Hgb at week 4.\n4. **Vitamin D**: 50,000 IU weekly x 6 weeks.\n5. **Oral nutritional supplements**: Ensure Plus or equivalent 1-2 cans daily between meals if not meeting caloric goals.\n6. **Immunonutrition**: Consider arginine/omega-3/nucleotide-enriched formula (Impact Advanced Recovery) for 5 days pre-surgery — evidence shows reduced infection rates in colorectal surgery.\n7. **Glycemic optimization**: Optimize diabetes management. Reduce HbA1c as much as possible in 6 weeks. Perioperative glucose <180 mg/dL target.\n\n**PSYCHOLOGICAL PREPARATION**:\n\n1. **Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)**: Weekly sessions addressing health anxiety, grief processing, and fear of cancer death. Specifically address his equation of his diagnosis with his wife\'s death (different cancer, different prognosis).\n2. **Discontinue diphenhydramine**: Replace with sleep hygiene protocol (consistent sleep/wake times, no screens 1 hour before bed, cool dark room) and melatonin 3 mg if needed.\n3. **Start SSRI**: Sertraline 50 mg daily (helps both depression and anxiety; no significant surgical interaction).\n4. **Relaxation training**: Diaphragmatic breathing, progressive muscle relaxation, guided imagery. Practice daily — will also use perioperatively for anxiety management.\n5. **Social reconnection**: Encourage resumption of one social activity per week. Walking the dog counts — both exercise and mood benefit.\n6. **Preoperative teaching**: Familiarize with what to expect post-surgery (pain management, early mobilization protocol, stoma education if applicable). Reduces anxiety and improves compliance.\n\n**MONITORING AND REASSESSMENT**:\n- 6MWT, grip strength, chair stand, TUG at baseline, week 3, and week 6 (pre-surgery)\n- Weekly weights, biweekly labs (Hgb, prealbumin, glucose)\n- PHQ-9 and GAD-7 at weeks 3 and 6\n- Communicate results to surgical team — if significant functional improvement, advocate for proceeding with surgery. If functional decline, alert team to higher perioperative risk.',
    ),
