    Section(
      title: '+ Address insurance justification for prehabilitation, the safety of a 6-week surgical delay for prehabilitation, and the emerging prehabilitation service delivery models.',
      content:
          '**Insurance Justification**:\n\n1. **Diagnosis coding**: Use ICD-10 codes that support medical necessity — R53.83 (other fatigue), M62.84 (sarcopenia), E44.1 (mild protein-calorie malnutrition), Z51.89 (encounter for other specified aftercare), and the cancer diagnosis (C18.2, malignant neoplasm of ascending colon).\n2. **Therapy justification**: Frame as medical rehabilitation for documented functional impairments (reduced 6MWT, impaired grip strength, abnormal TUG, sarcopenia) rather than "exercise." Impairment-based documentation with specific functional goals and measurable outcomes.\n3. **Prior authorization strategy**: Include surgeon\'s letter stating that functional optimization is medically necessary prior to major abdominal surgery. Cite complication reduction data and reduced length-of-stay evidence.\n4. **Coverage reality**: Most commercial insurance covers PT/OT for documented functional impairments. Medicare covers therapy with documented medical necessity. Cancer prehabilitation as a formal program is gaining recognition but is not yet a universally covered benefit category. Individual therapy sessions (PT, OT, psychology) are covered under existing benefit structures.\n\n**Safety of 6-Week Surgical Delay**:\n\n1. **Oncologic safety**: For Stage III colon cancer, the standard recommendation is surgery within 4-8 weeks of diagnosis. A 6-week prehabilitation window falls within standard surgical scheduling timelines and does not represent a delay beyond normal practice.\n2. **Evidence**: Multiple studies show that a 4-6 week delay for prehabilitation in colorectal cancer does NOT worsen oncologic outcomes (no difference in overall survival or disease-free survival).\n3. **Risk-benefit**: The patient\'s current functional status (6MWT 285 m, sarcopenia, anemia, malnutrition) places him at HIGH risk for postoperative complications. Prehabilitation to even modestly improve these parameters likely reduces complication risk more than any potential harm from a standard-timeline delay.\n4. **Communication with surgeon**: Present objective data — baseline functional tests, anemia severity, nutritional parameters — and projected improvement trajectory. Frame as a shared goal of getting the patient to surgery in the best possible condition.\n\n**Emerging Prehabilitation Service Delivery Models**:\n\n1. **Hospital-based prehabilitation programs**: Dedicated clinics within cancer centers (e.g., McGill Prehabilitation Program model). Multidisciplinary team (physiatrist, PT, dietitian, psychologist) under one roof.\n2. **Home-based/hybrid programs**: Initial in-person assessment, then supervised home exercise via telehealth with wearable activity monitors. Particularly useful for patients in rural areas (like this patient\'s daughter\'s suggestion).\n3. **Community-based partnerships**: Partner with local gyms, YMCAs (LIVESTRONG at the YMCA program), or community centers for supervised exercise.\n4. **Digital prehabilitation**: App-based exercise programs with remote monitoring. Emerging evidence supports feasibility and adherence, though less robust than supervised programs.\n5. **Prehabilitation as a quality metric**: Some institutions are incorporating prehabilitation into Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) protocols as a standard preoperative pathway, increasing institutional support and funding.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          'When you present the prehabilitation plan, the patient stares at the floor and says quietly: "My wife had cancer too. She did everything right — all the treatments, all the exercises they told her to do. And she still died in eight months. So tell me, doctor, why should I bother exercising when I might die anyway?"',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ How do you respond to this patient\'s existential distress and motivate engagement in prehabilitation while honoring his grief?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Pause. Do not rush past this moment.**\n[Sit quietly for a moment. Make eye contact. This is not a question that needs an immediate clinical answer — it is an expression of grief and existential fear.]\n\n**2. Honor his wife\'s memory and his loss**:\n\'I am truly sorry about your wife. Losing your partner to cancer — and now facing a cancer diagnosis yourself — is an extraordinary burden. I cannot imagine how that must feel. Would you tell me a little about her?\'\n[Allow him to share. This validates that she matters in this conversation and that you see him as a person, not just a patient.]\n\n**3. Acknowledge the fear without dismissing it**:\n\'Your fear makes perfect sense. You watched the person you love most go through treatment and not survive, and now you are being asked to do similar things. It would be strange if you were not afraid. I would be afraid too.\'\n\n**4. Gently differentiate without minimizing**:\n\'I want to be honest with you. Pancreatic cancer and colon cancer are very different diseases. Your wife\'s cancer is one of the most aggressive cancers we know. Your colon cancer, caught at Stage III, has a very different trajectory. With surgery and chemotherapy, the majority of patients with your stage of colon cancer — roughly 70 percent — are alive and well at five years. I do not want to make promises I cannot keep, but the odds here are genuinely in your favor.\'\n\n**5. Reframe exercise not as a cure, but as an investment in his experience**:\n\'Here is how I think about prehabilitation. Even if I cannot guarantee you a specific outcome — and no doctor can — what I can offer you is this: patients who are stronger going into surgery recover faster, spend less time in the hospital, have fewer complications, and feel better sooner. Exercise will not change the biology of your cancer, but it will change how you experience the next six months of your life. That matters whether you live five more years or fifty.\'\n\n**6. Connect to what he still loves**:\n\'You mentioned your dog — your golden retriever. You said he is your companion. Right now, you cannot walk him like you used to, and that is a loss too. What if we started there? What if the first goal of prehabilitation is simply getting you back to walking your dog for 20 minutes? Not because of surgery, not because of cancer — just because it is something that brings you joy.\'\n\n**7. Offer the relationship, not just the program**:\n\'I am not going to force you into anything. But I would like you to come back next week — just to talk, and maybe try a few exercises. If it feels pointless after two or three sessions, we will have an honest conversation about that. But I think you owe it to yourself to try before deciding it is not worth it. And I will be here either way.\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Allowing silence and emotional space, honoring the deceased spouse, differentiating prognosis without dismissing fear, reframing motivation from survival to quality of experience, connecting to intrinsic values (the dog), offering unconditional therapeutic relationship, avoiding false reassurance while providing realistic hope.',
    ),
